  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Solve the following by forming a quadratic equation.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The length of a square prism is 1 cm longer than the height and the width.\nThe surface area is $66\\text{ cm}^2$.\nWhat are the dimensions of the prism?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3\\text{ cm}\\times3\\text{ cm}\\times4\\text{ cm}$. Use $2x^2+4x(x+1)=66$, with $x>0$.",
          "worked": "Let the height and width be $x$ cm, so the length is $(x+1)$ cm and $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+4x(x+1)&=66\\\\6x^2+4x-66&=0\\\\3x^2+2x-33&=0\\end{align*}$$\nFor the quadratic formula, $a=3$, $b=2$, $c=-33$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(3)(-33)}}{2(3)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-2\\pm20}{6}\\\\x&=3\\text{ or }-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nReject the negative length. The dimensions are $3\\text{ cm}\\times3\\text{ cm}\\times4\\text{ cm}$.\nCheck: $2(3\\times3+3\\times4+3\\times4)=66\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The length, width, and height of a rectangular prism are consecutive numbers.\nThe surface area is $214\\text{ cm}^2$.\nWhat are the dimensions of the prism?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$5\\text{ cm}\\times6\\text{ cm}\\times7\\text{ cm}$. Use $2[x(x+1)+x(x+2)+(x+1)(x+2)]=214$.",
          "worked": "Let the dimensions be $x$, $x+1$ and $x+2$ cm, with $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2[x(x+1)+x(x+2)+(x+1)(x+2)]&=214\\\\6x^2+12x+4&=214\\\\6x^2+12x-210&=0\\\\x^2+2x-35&=0\\end{align*}$$\nFor the quadratic formula, $a=1$, $b=2$, $c=-35$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(-35)}}{2(1)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-2\\pm12}{2}\\\\x&=5\\text{ or }-7\\end{align*}$$\nReject the negative length. The dimensions are $5\\text{ cm}\\times6\\text{ cm}\\times7\\text{ cm}$.\nCheck: $2(5\\times6+5\\times7+6\\times7)=214\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 95
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "A square prism has height 1 cm less than the length (and width).\nThe surface area is $80\\text{ cm}^2$.\nWhat are the dimensions of the prism?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4\\text{ cm}\\times4\\text{ cm}\\times3\\text{ cm}$. Use $2x^2+4x(x-1)=80$, with $x>1$.",
          "worked": "Let the length and width be $x$ cm, so the height is $(x-1)$ cm and $x>1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+4x(x-1)&=80\\\\6x^2-4x-80&=0\\\\3x^2-2x-40&=0\\end{align*}$$\nFor the quadratic formula, $a=3$, $b=-2$, $c=-40$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-(-2)\\pm\\sqrt{(-2)^2-4(3)(-40)}}{2(3)}\\\\x&=\\frac{2\\pm22}{6}\\\\x&=4\\text{ or }-\\frac{10}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nReject the negative length. The dimensions are $4\\text{ cm}\\times4\\text{ cm}\\times3\\text{ cm}$.\nCheck: $2(4\\times4+4\\times3+4\\times3)=80\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The height of a rectangular prism is 1 cm more than the width. The length is double the height.\nThe surface area of the cuboid is $72\\text{ cm}^2$.\nWhat are the dimensions of the prism?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\text{ cm}\\times3\\text{ cm}\\times6\\text{ cm}$ (width, height, length). Use $2[x(x+1)+2x(x+1)+2(x+1)^2]=72$.",
          "worked": "Let the width be $x$ cm, so the height is $(x+1)$ cm and the length is $2(x+1)$ cm, with $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2[x(x+1)+2x(x+1)+2(x+1)^2]&=72\\\\10x^2+14x+4&=72\\\\10x^2+14x-68&=0\\\\5x^2+7x-34&=0\\end{align*}$$\nFor the quadratic formula, $a=5$, $b=7$, $c=-34$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-7\\pm\\sqrt{7^2-4(5)(-34)}}{2(5)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-7\\pm27}{10}\\\\x&=2\\text{ or }-\\frac{17}{5}\\end{align*}$$\nReject the negative length. The width, height and length are $2$ cm, $3$ cm and $6$ cm respectively.\nCheck: $2(2\\times3+2\\times6+3\\times6)=72\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 95
      }
    ]
  },
